/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The acceptance derivation ended with n₀ sin θa = √(n₁² − n₂²).
   - NA is dimensionless; n₀ is the index outside the end face (air, 1); θa is the acceptance half-angle; n₁ and n₂ are the core and cladding indices.
   - NA does not depend on the outside medium: put the fiber in water and θa shrinks, but n₀ sin θa stays the same.
   - The relative index difference Δ expresses the index step as a fraction.
   - The approximation uses n₁² − n₂² = (n₁ + n₂)(n₁ − n₂) ≈ 2n₁(n₁ − n₂), valid when n₁ and n₂ are close (weak guidance, Δ ≪ 1).
   - Rearranging the exact form gives n₁² − n₂² = 2n₁²Δ, so NA = n₁√(2Δ).
   - A meridional ray lies in a plane that contains the fiber axis.
   - A skew ray enters off the axis and never crosses it.
   - In a straight step-index fiber every reflection of a given skew ray happens at the same angle of incidence, so a skew ray that meets the TIR condition at one reflection meets it at all of them.
   - n₀ sin θas · cos γ = √(n₁² − n₂²) = NA θas is the acceptance angle for a skew ray whose end-on projection makes angle γ with the radius.
*/
