/* book exam traps: The slab V uses the full thickness d here, with cutoffs at mπ. If your book puts d/2 in V, the cutoffs are mπ/2. Mixing the two halves or doubles the answer. | floor(V/π) + 1 counts one family (TE). Counting TE and TM together doubles it; do not double again 'for polarization', because TE and TM already are the two polarizations. | Mode m appears when V passes mπ: TE₁ appears at V = π, not 2π. A symmetric slab with V just below π is single-mode. | A guided mode needs n₂ < neff < n₁. A value below n₂ means the field radiates into the cladding. | A fiber uses its core radius a and the limit 2.405; a slab uses thickness d and the limit π. Do not carry one rule into the other. */
